 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** Connected browsers receive revisions over WebSocket sessions operated by MarketBoard. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it adds delivery lag and repeated reads. A hosted realtime service was rejected because it would couple the board to a provider and add recurring vendor cost. This choice requires the system to operate connection capacity, reconnection, replay, and reliable publication. See [the delivery decision](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md) for the options and selection criteria.
